.TH LUAN 1 "2026" "luan 1.2" "Luan Manual"
.SH NAME
luan \- minimalist text templating and macro engine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B luan
.B \-\-file
.I TEMPLATE
[\fB\-\-vars\fR \fIVARFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-out\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-dump\-vars\fR]
.br
.B luan
[\fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-\-banner\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B luan
is a single-file, zero-dependency text macro engine. It takes a
.I template
file containing plain text mixed with variable substitutions and a
small set of block directives, resolves a variable
.I scope
from three layered sources, and renders the result to stdout or to a
file.

It is not a general-purpose templating library. There is no
expression language beyond simple defaults and integer +/\- arithmetic,
no user-defined functions, and no nested data structures. Lists are
flat, comma-separated strings. This is deliberate: luan is meant to
be readable end-to-end in one sitting, both the engine and the
templates it renders.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Template file to render. Required for rendering; omitted only when
using \fB\-\-version\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR, or \fB\-\-banner\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-vars " " \fIFILE\fR
A key=value file merged into the variable scope before rendering.
One assignment per line. Lines starting with
.B #
and blank lines are ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-vars
Resolve the template's variable scope \(em including any
.B @set
directives inside it \(em and print it as JSON to stdout.
.B No template output is produced.
Useful for debugging a scope before trusting the rendered output.
.TP
.BR \-\-out " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the rendered result to \fIFILE\fR instead of stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-banner
Print the luan ASCII banner and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version number and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH VARIABLE RESOLUTION ORDER
A variable may be defined in more than one place. The first
definition found wins, in this order:
.IP 1. 4
.B @set
directives inside the template (evaluated top to bottom as the
template is walked; a later
.B @set
of the same key overrides an earlier one).
.IP 2. 4
The
.B \-\-vars
file, if given.
.IP 3. 4
Environment variables prefixed
.BR LUAN_ ,
lowercased with the prefix stripped. For example
.B LUAN_AUTHOR=lay
in the environment makes
.B {{author}}
resolve to
.BR lay .
.SH TEMPLATE SYNTAX
.SS Comments
.PP
A line whose first two characters are
.B #!
is dropped entirely from the output.
.IP
.EX
#! this line never appears in the render
.EE
.SS Substitution
.PP
.B {{name}}
is replaced with the current value of
.I name
in scope. If
.I name
is undefined, the literal text
.B {{name}}
is left in place \(em unresolved substitutions are visible on
purpose, so a broken template fails loudly instead of silently
dropping content.
.IP
.EX
Hello, {{author}}!
.EE
.SS Defaults
.PP
.B {{name|default}}
falls back to the literal text
.I default
if
.I name
is unset or empty.
.IP
.EX
{{nickname|anonymous}}
.EE
.SS Arithmetic
.PP
.B {{name+N}}
or
.B {{name\-N}}
adds or subtracts the integer
.I N
from the current numeric value of
.IR name .
Only single-digit-or-more integer literals are supported, and only
one operation per expression \(em there is no operator chaining and
no parentheses.
.IP
.EX
@set year = 2026
Next year: {{year+1}}
.EE
.SS @set
.PP
.B @set key = value
defines or overrides a variable within the template itself. The
right-hand side is itself substituted before assignment, so a
.B @set
may reference earlier variables.
.IP
.EX
@set first = Ada
@set greeting = Hello, {{first}}
{{greeting}}!
.EE
.SS @if / @end
.PP
.B @if key
opens a conditional block, closed by a matching
.BR @end .
The block is rendered only when
.I key
is
.IR truthy .
A key is falsy when it is undefined, empty, the literal string
.BR false ,
or the literal string
.BR 0 ;
anything else is truthy.
.IP
.EX
@if premium
Thanks for being a premium user.
@end
.EE
.SS @each / @end
.PP
.B @each item in list
opens a loop block, closed by a matching
.BR @end .
.I list
must resolve to a comma-separated string; each comma-separated
element becomes the value of
.I item
for one pass through the block body, in order, with surrounding
whitespace trimmed. Empty elements are skipped.
.IP
.EX
@set stack = C, Go, Ruby, Perl
@each lang in stack
\- {{lang}}
@end
.EE
.PP
.B @if
and
.B @each
blocks may be nested inside one another; each
.B @end
closes the nearest still-open block.
.SS @include
.PP
.B @include path
inlines another template file at this point, rendered against the
.B same
variable scope. The path is resolved relative to the directory of
the file containing the
.B @include
directive, not the current working directory \(em so includes work
the same way regardless of where
.B luan
is invoked from.
.IP
.EX
@include partials/footer.luan
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful render, or a \fB\-\-help\fR/\fB\-\-version\fR/\fB\-\-banner\fR call.
.TP
.B 1
Template file not found, vars file not found, an
.B @if
or
.B @each
block missing its matching
.BR @end ,
or an unknown command-line argument.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I *.luan
Conventional extension for luan template files. Not enforced by the
engine \(em any file path works.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Render a template with an external variable file:
.IP
.EX
luan \-\-file report.luan \-\-vars prod.vars \-\-out report.txt
.EE
.PP
Inspect the resolved scope without rendering, to catch a typo before
it ships:
.IP
.EX
luan \-\-file report.luan \-\-vars prod.vars \-\-dump\-vars
.EE
.PP
Feed a variable through the environment instead of a vars file:
.IP
.EX
LUAN_AUTHOR=lay luan \-\-file letter.luan
.EE
.SH NOTES
.PP
There is intentionally no
.BR @else .
An
.B @if
that needs an alternate branch is usually a sign the logic belongs
in whatever generates the
.B \-\-vars
file, not in the template. Keep the template dumb; keep the
variables smart.
.SH AUTHOR
written by lay(translate by claude).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR m4 (1),
.BR envsubst (1)
